1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,150
What is time? At first glance it is one
of the simplest things in the world.

2
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:07,627
We have clocks. We have calendars.

3
00:00:08,577 --> 00:00:10,067
We know what time it is.

4
00:00:10,797 --> 00:00:14,187
We know that yesterday was yesterday
and that tomorrow will be tomorrow.

5
00:00:14,998 --> 00:00:17,948
But stop for a moment and ask
yourself a simple question.

6
00:00:18,898 --> 00:00:20,328
What is time, actually?

7
00:00:21,118 --> 00:00:22,968
And suddenly it is much stranger.

8
00:00:23,802 --> 00:00:27,342
Because we may be struggling to separate
two completely different things.

9
00:00:27,422 --> 00:00:31,112
Time itself. And our
feeling that time flows.

10
00:00:32,049 --> 00:00:36,089
And that one distinction takes us from
ordinary clocks to Einstein, to entropy,

11
00:00:36,169 --> 00:00:40,959
to the brain, and to the question of
whether there is any objective now at all.

12
00:00:43,499 --> 00:00:46,789
When we say that an hour has
passed, what has actually happened?

13
00:00:47,719 --> 00:00:49,769
A clock ticked a certain number of times.

14
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,790
An atomic clock counted the transitions
between the energy states of an atom.

15
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:57,810
A pendulum swung back and forth.

16
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,790
A planet turned through a certain angle.

17
00:01:01,489 --> 00:01:05,139
But notice something.
Clocks do not create time.

18
00:01:05,869 --> 00:01:09,019
They only compare one regular
change against another change.

19
00:01:09,995 --> 00:01:13,245
We say, for instance, that
an event lasted ten seconds.

20
00:01:13,395 --> 00:01:16,045
And what we are really saying is this.

21
00:01:16,834 --> 00:01:22,024
During that event, as many regular changes
went past as ten seconds are made of.

22
00:01:22,711 --> 00:01:25,721
So in everyday life we come
to know time through change.

23
00:01:26,711 --> 00:01:28,801
And this is where it
starts to get interesting.

24
00:01:31,651 --> 00:01:34,981
Before Einstein, physics
pictured time very simply.

25
00:01:35,811 --> 00:01:39,781
Newton imagined time as something
that exists entirely on its own.

26
00:01:40,539 --> 00:01:43,669
The universe can change. Objects can move.

27
00:01:43,859 --> 00:01:48,699
But somewhere in the background there
is a universal clock. Tick. Tick. Tick.

28
00:01:48,779 --> 00:01:52,009
And it ticks in exactly
the same way for everybody.

29
00:01:52,841 --> 00:01:55,751
Time was supposed to be
a vast invisible river,

30
00:01:55,921 --> 00:01:58,871
flowing through the whole
universe at one steady rate.

31
00:01:59,572 --> 00:02:01,762
But Einstein turned up with a problem.

32
00:02:02,572 --> 00:02:04,462
And the problem was the speed of light.

33
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:07,926
Under special relativity,
light in a vacuum

34
00:02:08,006 --> 00:02:10,536
has the same speed for every observer.

35
00:02:11,546 --> 00:02:13,796
And that leads to
something completely mad.

36
00:02:14,573 --> 00:02:16,723
Time is not the same for everyone.

37
00:02:17,493 --> 00:02:20,153
If two observers move at
different speeds relative to

38
00:02:20,233 --> 00:02:23,203
each other, they can measure
different intervals of time.

39
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:26,443
So time is not a universal backdrop.

40
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:29,143
It is part of the physical system.

41
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:31,830
And here is the strange part.

42
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:36,110
Each of the two will say that it is
the other one's clock that runs slow.

43
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,430
Not their own. The other one's.

44
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:40,861
It is not an optical illusion.

45
00:02:41,611 --> 00:02:43,301
It is not a fault in the clocks.

46
00:02:44,091 --> 00:02:46,841
Time really does pass differently
for different observers.

47
00:02:47,271 --> 00:02:49,381
And that was only the beginning.

48
00:02:52,221 --> 00:02:56,291
Einstein's relativity joined space and
time into a single object. Spacetime.

49
00:02:58,461 --> 00:03:01,651
So we do not simply have
space and time side by side.

50
00:03:01,961 --> 00:03:03,431
We have spacetime.

51
00:03:03,981 --> 00:03:05,891
And that has one very odd consequence.

52
00:03:06,943 --> 00:03:09,793
When we ask what happened at
the same time as some event,

53
00:03:10,123 --> 00:03:12,873
the answer need not be the
same for every observer.

54
00:03:13,653 --> 00:03:16,723
Picture two people moving
quickly relative to each other.

55
00:03:17,453 --> 00:03:19,683
They can agree that two events took place.

56
00:03:20,493 --> 00:03:24,263
But they need not agree on whether those
events happened at the same moment.

57
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:28,448
And that brings us to one of the
strangest ideas in all of physics.

58
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:31,128
There is no universal now.

59
00:03:33,738 --> 00:03:38,508
We feel that there is one single moment
that is genuinely present. Right now.

60
00:03:38,878 --> 00:03:42,028
You are watching this
video. This is the present.

61
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:46,190
But special relativity tells
us there is no single universal

62
00:03:46,270 --> 00:03:49,800
slice of the present that
holds for the whole universe.

63
00:03:50,681 --> 00:03:53,461
What counts as now for
me need not count as now

64
00:03:53,541 --> 00:03:56,991
for an observer far away
who is moving very fast.

65
00:03:57,859 --> 00:04:01,869
And here a rather interesting
philosophical possibility opens up.

66
00:04:02,539 --> 00:04:05,159
Perhaps the universe is not
something that keeps changing

67
00:04:05,239 --> 00:04:08,389
from the past, through the
present, into the future.

68
00:04:09,136 --> 00:04:12,826
Perhaps reality is more like a
four-dimensional structure of events.

69
00:04:13,816 --> 00:04:16,086
This view is often called
the block universe.

70
00:04:16,999 --> 00:04:21,139
But be careful. Relativity on
its own does not prove that the

71
00:04:21,219 --> 00:04:23,769
block universe is definitely
the right description.

72
00:04:24,735 --> 00:04:28,985
It is an interpretation that follows very
naturally from the relativistic picture.

73
00:04:29,915 --> 00:04:32,195
And if it is right,
then our idea of flowing

74
00:04:32,275 --> 00:04:34,885
time starts to look very strange indeed.

75
00:04:37,835 --> 00:04:40,665
So why do we experience
time as a one-way street?

76
00:04:41,455 --> 00:04:44,505
Here is something everybody
knows. A broken egg.

77
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,057
Drop an egg on the floor and it breaks.

78
00:04:47,927 --> 00:04:50,027
But you never see the pieces
gather themselves back

79
00:04:50,107 --> 00:04:54,077
up and the egg leap off the
ground into your hand. Why not?

80
00:04:54,927 --> 00:04:56,817
Because of something we call entropy.

81
00:04:57,967 --> 00:05:00,607
Very roughly, a system
has an enormous number of

82
00:05:00,687 --> 00:05:04,617
untidy states available to
it, and far fewer tidy ones.

83
00:05:05,435 --> 00:05:08,065
A whole egg is one very particular state.

84
00:05:09,015 --> 00:05:12,785
Shards on the floor can be arranged
in an unimaginable number of ways.

85
00:05:13,661 --> 00:05:17,611
So it is far more likely that a system
moves from order towards disorder.

86
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:20,631
And that is how we get the arrow of time.

87
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:25,881
But wait. That cannot be the whole story.

88
00:05:26,071 --> 00:05:29,821
Because entropy does not increase
everywhere. Look around you.

89
00:05:30,511 --> 00:05:33,481
Crystals form. Stars form. Planets form.

90
00:05:33,771 --> 00:05:35,841
And eventually… things like us form.

91
00:05:36,583 --> 00:05:40,573
Life is an extraordinarily
complex, highly structured system.

92
00:05:41,503 --> 00:05:43,543
So how can something
that ordered exist in a

93
00:05:43,623 --> 00:05:46,493
universe that is supposedly
moving towards disorder?

94
00:05:47,262 --> 00:05:51,192
Because the second law does not say
that entropy must increase everywhere.

95
00:05:52,142 --> 00:05:56,392
It says that for an isolated system,
the total entropy does not decrease.

96
00:05:57,259 --> 00:06:01,449
The Earth takes in energy from the
Sun in a fairly concentrated form.

97
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:04,320
And it eventually sends
that energy back out

98
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:07,230
into space as more
dispersed thermal radiation.

99
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,510
In between, life gets to
do interesting things.

100
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:17,530
And now one of the loveliest
paradoxes in the whole of science.

101
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:20,900
Many of the basic laws
of physics work almost

102
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:23,570
equally well when we run time backwards.

103
00:06:24,495 --> 00:06:28,785
Picture a film of planets going round
their orbits. Play it in reverse.

104
00:06:29,975 --> 00:06:32,325
At first glance nothing
looks wrong at all.

105
00:06:33,022 --> 00:06:35,612
Many basic equations behave the same way.

106
00:06:36,222 --> 00:06:40,192
They do not say: here is the
past. Here is the future.

107
00:06:40,762 --> 00:06:43,432
They never say that time
must run in this direction.

108
00:06:44,602 --> 00:06:46,792
So where does our arrow of time come from?

109
00:06:47,462 --> 00:06:50,172
One of the main explanations
is entropy again.

110
00:06:51,082 --> 00:06:54,692
Our universe began in a state
of extraordinarily low entropy.

111
00:06:55,560 --> 00:07:00,090
And from that very odd starting point,
entropy has been rising ever since.

112
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,090
That creates a direction which we
experience as past, and then future.

113
00:07:06,047 --> 00:07:08,557
And here we run into
an even deeper problem.

114
00:07:09,427 --> 00:07:12,557
The second law may explain
why entropy keeps increasing.

115
00:07:13,399 --> 00:07:16,989
But only if we already start with
a universe in a very special state.

116
00:07:17,959 --> 00:07:20,449
A state of extraordinarily low entropy.

117
00:07:21,305 --> 00:07:26,635
So perhaps the real question is not why
entropy increases. Perhaps it is this.

118
00:07:27,045 --> 00:07:30,355
Why was the universe ever so
ordered in the first place?

119
00:07:33,265 --> 00:07:35,435
We do not yet have a complete answer.

120
00:07:36,065 --> 00:07:38,075
We thought we were asking what time is.

121
00:07:38,888 --> 00:07:41,558
But now we have found an
even stranger question.

122
00:07:42,388 --> 00:07:44,778
Why does the universe
have a direction at all?

123
00:07:47,728 --> 00:07:51,058
But entropy alone does not
explain our whole feeling of time.

124
00:07:51,308 --> 00:07:55,418
Because we do not only measure
time. We live through it.

125
00:07:56,148 --> 00:07:58,588
We feel that the past has
already been, that the present

126
00:07:58,668 --> 00:08:03,098
is happening now, and that the
future is still on its way. Why?

127
00:08:03,945 --> 00:08:05,915
One important reason is memory.

128
00:08:06,765 --> 00:08:08,375
We hold records of the past.

129
00:08:08,985 --> 00:08:10,815
We hold no memories of the future.

130
00:08:11,725 --> 00:08:14,765
When I remember yesterday's
breakfast, my brain holds

131
00:08:14,845 --> 00:08:17,655
information about an event
that really happened.

132
00:08:18,456 --> 00:08:22,866
But I hold no equally available record
of what I am going to eat tomorrow.

133
00:08:23,976 --> 00:08:26,426
And that creates a very
powerful asymmetry.

134
00:08:27,151 --> 00:08:31,111
The brain is constantly taking
information in, storing some of it,

135
00:08:31,191 --> 00:08:34,901
comparing the new against the old,
and predicting what comes next.

136
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:38,440
And out of that process
comes our subjective feeling.

137
00:08:39,250 --> 00:08:41,320
I am moving through time right now.

138
00:08:43,670 --> 00:08:45,260
And there is another problem.

139
00:08:46,070 --> 00:08:50,160
Our awareness cannot take in the whole
world in a single mathematical instant.

140
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:53,674
The brain needs time
to process information.

141
00:08:54,584 --> 00:08:56,974
A signal from the eye
has to reach the brain.

142
00:08:57,724 --> 00:08:59,494
The brain has to work on it.

143
00:09:00,188 --> 00:09:02,778
It has to compare it
against other information.

144
00:09:03,708 --> 00:09:06,178
And only then does a
conscious perception appear.

145
00:09:07,049 --> 00:09:09,699
So our now is not a perfectly sharp point.

146
00:09:10,549 --> 00:09:13,619
It is more like the outcome of
an enormous amount of processing.

147
00:09:14,784 --> 00:09:19,054
The brain builds a model of reality that
is consistent enough for us to function.

148
00:09:19,144 --> 00:09:22,454
And that model contains
something we call the present.

149
00:09:25,124 --> 00:09:27,834
And we can watch this
happening every single day.

150
00:09:28,604 --> 00:09:30,854
We sit in a waiting room for two hours.

151
00:09:31,224 --> 00:09:33,154
And it feels like a whole day.

152
00:09:33,928 --> 00:09:38,138
Then we spend six hours playing a
brilliant game. We look at the clock.

153
00:09:39,048 --> 00:09:41,018
And we say: is it really that late?

154
00:09:41,879 --> 00:09:44,769
The clocks were working
in exactly the same way.

155
00:09:45,119 --> 00:09:46,649
The physics did not change.

156
00:09:47,479 --> 00:09:49,789
What changed was our experience of time.

157
00:09:50,597 --> 00:09:53,567
Attention, emotion, the
amount of new information,

158
00:09:53,957 --> 00:09:56,987
and memory all shape how
long a stretch of time feels.

159
00:09:57,776 --> 00:10:00,546
So when we say that the
day went by terribly fast,

160
00:10:00,776 --> 00:10:03,406
we may not be talking
about physical time at all.

161
00:10:03,896 --> 00:10:07,006
We are talking about our model
of time inside the brain.

162
00:10:09,616 --> 00:10:11,126
And here we should be careful.

163
00:10:11,876 --> 00:10:14,946
Saying that time is merely
an illusion is far too easy.

164
00:10:15,687 --> 00:10:17,997
Time is not something we simply made up.

165
00:10:18,767 --> 00:10:21,137
Relativistic effects on time are real.

166
00:10:21,927 --> 00:10:23,317
Atomic clocks measure them.

167
00:10:24,001 --> 00:10:26,671
Satellite navigation
has to correct for them.

168
00:10:26,981 --> 00:10:30,291
Time is part of our most
successful physical theories.

169
00:10:31,203 --> 00:10:34,873
So time is certainly not just
an invention of the human brain.

170
00:10:35,303 --> 00:10:37,713
But something else may
well be an illusion.

171
00:10:38,597 --> 00:10:40,587
Our idea that time really flows.

172
00:10:40,877 --> 00:10:45,967
And perhaps also our idea that
there is one universal moment. Now.

173
00:10:48,657 --> 00:10:53,657
Picture a whole life. Childhood. School.
A first love. Work. Family. Today.

174
00:10:53,737 --> 00:10:56,487
And one day, the last
moments of that life.

175
00:10:57,297 --> 00:11:00,587
We experience all of it as a journey
from the past into the future.

176
00:11:01,448 --> 00:11:03,348
But it may be more
accurate to picture a whole

177
00:11:03,428 --> 00:11:06,778
life as a sequence of events in spacetime.

178
00:11:07,268 --> 00:11:10,118
Every moment has its own
place in that structure.

179
00:11:11,061 --> 00:11:14,001
And our awareness always holds
information about some earlier

180
00:11:14,081 --> 00:11:17,951
states, and builds expectations
of the states still to come.

181
00:11:18,666 --> 00:11:22,196
And that may be exactly what creates
the feeling that time is flowing.

182
00:11:23,226 --> 00:11:28,056
This does not mean that we know that
time does not flow. We do not know that.

183
00:11:28,857 --> 00:11:33,007
It is one of the deep open problems on
the border between physics and philosophy.

184
00:11:35,997 --> 00:11:40,947
So what is time? Perhaps the best
answer is a surprisingly modest one.

185
00:11:41,437 --> 00:11:42,967
We do not entirely know.

186
00:11:43,691 --> 00:11:46,051
In modern physics, time
is part of the structure

187
00:11:46,131 --> 00:11:49,541
that describes events and
the relations between them.

188
00:11:50,209 --> 00:11:53,059
Relativity showed us
that it is not universal.

189
00:11:54,089 --> 00:11:57,859
Thermodynamics gives us the arrow
of time through rising entropy.

190
00:11:58,712 --> 00:12:02,012
And our brain builds, out of
those physical processes, a

191
00:12:02,092 --> 00:12:05,802
subjective experience of the
past, the present and the future.

192
00:12:06,573 --> 00:12:09,953
So perhaps there are two quite
different things. Physical time.

193
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:11,383
And lived time.

194
00:12:11,813 --> 00:12:13,823
And they need not be the same thing.

195
00:12:16,383 --> 00:12:18,803
So the next time you glance
at a clock and read the hour

196
00:12:18,883 --> 00:12:22,653
off its face, you might remember
what it is not showing you.

197
00:12:23,285 --> 00:12:26,155
There is no invisible river
running through the universe.

198
00:12:26,665 --> 00:12:29,655
The clock shows you a
relation between changes.

199
00:12:30,508 --> 00:12:33,358
And your brain turns that
into something far stranger.

200
00:12:34,348 --> 00:12:36,828
The feeling that there is a
past, that there is a right

201
00:12:36,908 --> 00:12:40,118
now, and that the future is
still waiting ahead of us.

202
00:12:40,846 --> 00:12:44,436
And perhaps the greatest mystery
is not where time is flowing to.

203
00:12:44,926 --> 00:12:48,216
It is why we feel that it
is flowing anywhere at all.

204
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:52,721
And if that feeling is only the way
our brain reads change, memory and

205
00:12:52,801 --> 00:12:57,071
entropy, then the strangest thing
about time may not be that it exists.

206
00:12:58,021 --> 00:13:00,431
It is that we can experience it at all.
